{"answerArea":{"calculator":false,"chi2Table":false,"periodicTable":false,"tTable":false,"zTable":false},"hints":[{"content":"First, we note that $q=-\\dfrac32$ makes the denominator zero and so cannot be a solution.\n\nNow, we multiply both sides of the equation by $2q+3$ to get rid of the quotient. ","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{}},{"content":"$\\begin{align}\n\\dfrac{2}{2q + 3} \u0026= \\dfrac{1}{8}\n\\\\\\\\\n(2q+3)\\cdot \\dfrac{2}{2q + 3} \u0026= (2q+3)\\cdot \\dfrac{1}{8}\n\\\\\\\\\n2\u0026=\\dfrac28 q+\\dfrac38\n\\\\\\\\\n\\dfrac{13}{8}\u0026=\\dfrac28q\n\\\\\\\\\n\\dfrac{13}{2}\u0026=q\n\\end{align}$","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{}},{"content":"We get that $q=\\dfrac{13}{2}$ is a potential solution. It doesn't make the denominator zero, so it is indeed a solution.\n\n**In conclusion, $q = \\dfrac{13}{2}$.**","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{}}],"itemDataVersion":{"major":0,"minor":1},"question":{"content":"**Solve for $q$.**\n\n$\\dfrac{2}{2q + 3} = \\dfrac{1}{8}$\n\n$q =$ [[☃ numeric-input 1]]","images":{},"widgets":{"numeric-input 1":{"alignment":"default","graded":true,"options":{"answers":[{"maxError":0,"message":"","simplify":"required","status":"correct","strict":false,"value":6.5}],"coefficient":false,"labelText":"","multipleNumberInput":false,"size":"normal","static":false},"static":false,"type":"numeric-input","version":{"major":0,"minor":0}}}}}
